{% extends "layout.njk" %}

<article>{% block article %}
  <h3 id="css-utilities">CSS Utilities</h3>
  <p>
    Utilities allow you to quickly apply single CSS properties to an
    element directly within your HTML markup without having to write any
    code in a separate CSS file. Contrary to other CSS frameworks which
    use the class attribute, Startr Style is unique in that it applies
    utilities through CSS variables and the style attribute. This has a
    number of distinct improvements over class based utilities.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Use of all values</strong> - With class based utlities you
      can only assign a set number of values pre defined by the
      framework. Startr Style does not have this restraint. Any valid
      value for the CSS property can be applied.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Filesize</strong> - One of the biggest drawbacks to date
      with utility complete CSS frameworks is their large filesize. The
      developent version of Tailwind for example, minified is a rather
      large <strong>2413.4kB</strong>. Startr Style with the same
      feature set comes in at less than <strong>50kb</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h4 id="what-about-responsive">What about responsive</h4>
  <p>
    Startr Style utilities are perfect for developing fully responsive
    interfaces. Each utility can be applied conditionally to a
    screensize allowing you total responsive control.
  </p>
  <p>
    To apply a utility to a certain screensize simply append the utility
    shorthand with a &lsquo;-&rsquo; followed by the screen size.
    Utilities use a mobile-first approach so if you append with for
    example <code>-md</code>, that utility will be applied to the medium
    screensize and up.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the following example background is red by default and blue on
    large screen devices.
  </p>
  
  {% highlight "html" %}
<div style="--bg:red; --bg-lg:blue"><div>
  {% endhighlight %}
  
  <style>
    @media only screen and (max-width: 760px),
    (min-device-width: 768px) and (max-device-width: 1024px) {

      /* Force table to not be like tables anymore */
      table,
      thead,
      tbody,
      th,
      td,
      tr {
        display: block;
        min-width: 20rem;
      }

      /* Hide table headers (but not display: none;, for accessibility) */
      thead tr {
        position: absolute;
        top: -9999px;
        left: -9999px;
      }

      tr {
        /* margin: 0 0 1rem 0;*/
      }

      tr:nth-child(odd) {
        background: #ccc;
      }

      td {
        /* Behave  like a "row" */
        border: none;
        border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;
        position: relative;
        padding-left: 50%;
        min-height: 3rem;
      }

      td:before {
        /* Now like a table header */
        position: absolute;
        /* Top/left values mimic padding */
        top: 0.6rem;
        left: 6px;
        width: 45%;
        padding-right: 1rem;
        white-space: nowrap;
        content: attr(data-label);
      }
      .link-mdn {
       margin-top: 0.6rem;
      }
    }
  </style>

  {% if props and props.props and props.version == 1 %}
  <h4 id="tiers">Tiers</h4>
  <p>A tier is a suffix. It applies from its width up.</p>
  <ul>
    {% for name, px in props.breakpoints %}
    <li><code>-{{ name }}</code> applies from {{ px }}px up.</li>
    {% endfor %}
    <li><code>-pt</code> applies in print only.</li>
  </ul>

  <h4 id="states">States</h4>
  <p>A state is a suffix too, as in <code style="--ws: nowrap">--bg-hover</code>. A prop takes only the states its row lists.</p>
  {% set meaning = {
    "hover": "the element is hovered or keyboard-focused",
    "focus": "the element has keyboard focus (:focus-visible)",
    "active": "the element is pressed",
    "on": 'the element is on: aria-current except false or empty, aria-selected/pressed/expanded true, open, :checked, data-on except "false"',
    "in-on": "a direct child of an element that is on",
    "in-hover": "a direct child of an element that is hovered or keyboard-focused"
  } %}
  <ul>
    {% for state, text in meaning %}
    <li><strong>-{{ state }}</strong>: {{ text }}. Props: {% for name, entry in props.props %}{% if state in entry.states %}<code style="--ws: nowrap">{{ name }}-{{ state }}</code> {% endif %}{% endfor %}</li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
  <p>Later rules win: base, tier, in-hover, hover, in-on, on, focus, active. A state beats a tier. A current item ignores hover. Focus always shows.</p>

  <h4 id="helpers">Helpers</h4>
  <p>The newer helpers cover colour by number, motion and effects. Each line says what the helper sets, what else it reads, and shows an example.</p>
  {% set helper = {
    "--mix": {"text": "Three digits, each 0 to 5, pick one cell of the colour cube. 314 is red 3, green 1, blue 4.", "use": "--mix:314; --bg:var(--mixed, #7c61c1)"},
    "--tone": {"text": "A number from 0 to 100 mixes that share of --tone-of (--text-main by default) into --background, so greys and tints follow dark mode.", "use": "--tone:76; --b:1px solid var(--toned, #545454)"},
    "--anim": {"text": "Sets the whole animation shorthand, so a stagger rides inside it. Keyframes: st-fade, st-rise, st-slide-in, st-pop.", "use": "--anim:st-rise var(--dur-3) var(--ease-out) calc(var(--i, 0) * 45ms) both"},
    "--text-grad": {"text": "Fills the text with a gradient. It leaves the colour alone, so add --c:transparent.", "use": "--text-grad:linear-gradient(red, blue); --c:transparent"},
    "--glow": {"text": "Four growing shadow layers with no lift. --glow-c sets the colour, --primary by default.", "use": "--glow:8; --glow-c:var(--primary)"},
    "--bdf": {"text": "Filters what sits behind the element.", "use": "--bdf:blur(8px)"}
  } %}
  <ul>
    {% for name, h in helper %}{% set entry = props.props[name] %}{% if entry %}
    <li><code>{{ name }}</code>: {{ h.text }} Sets {{ entry.properties | join(", ") }}.{% if entry.reads %} Reads {{ entry.reads | join(", ") }}.{% endif %} Example: <code>{{ h.use }}</code></li>
    {% endif %}{% endfor %}
    {% set other = "" %}
    {% for name, entry in props.props %}{% if entry.kind == "helper" and not entry.deprecated and not helper[name] %}{% if not other %}
    <li>Other helpers: {% endif %}<code style="--ws: nowrap">{{ name }}</code> {% set other = "yes" %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% if other %}</li>{% endif %}
  </ul>

  <h4 id="deprecated">Deprecated</h4>
  <p>The old names keep working. New code should use the replacement.</p>
  <ul>
    {% for name, entry in props.props %}{% if entry.deprecated %}
    <li><code>{{ name }}</code> is replaced by <code>{{ entry.deprecated }}</code>.</li>
    {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  </ul>

  <h4 id="collisions">Collisions</h4>
  <p>A few props set two CSS properties from separate rules. Use the safer prop where there is one.</p>
  <ul>
    {% for name, entry in props.props %}{% if entry.collision %}
    <li><code>{{ name }}</code> sets {{ entry.collision | join(" and ") }}.{% for collision in entry.collision %}{% set found = "" %}
      For {{ collision }}, {% for otherName, other in props.props %}{% if other.kind == "prop" and not other.collision and not other.deprecated and other.properties | length == 1 and other.properties[0] == collision %}{% if found %}, {% else %}use {% endif %}<code>{{ otherName }}</code>{% set found = "yes" %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}{% if not found %}no safer prop yet{% endif %}.
    {% endfor %}</li>
    {% endif %}{% endfor %}
  </ul>

  <h4 id="all-props">All props</h4>
  <p>Every prop in the stylesheet, sorted by name. Add a tier or a state to the end of the name.</p>
  <table aria-label="Utilities">
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Shorthand</th><th>Property</th><th>Tiers</th><th>States</th><th>MDN</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for name, entry in props.props %}
      {% set mdn = "" %}
      {% for property in entry.properties %}{% if not mdn and property[0] != "-" %}{% set mdn = property %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
      <tr{% if name == "--p" %} id="padding"{% elif name == "--m" %} id="margin"{% endif %}>
        <td data-label="Shorthand"><span style="--weight: bold; --us: all" class="shorthand">{{ name }}</span>{% if entry.requires_suffix %} <small>suffix required</small>{% endif %}{% if entry.deprecated %} <small>deprecated, use {{ entry.deprecated }}</small>{% endif %}{% if entry.collision %} <small><a href="#collisions">collision</a></small>{% endif %}</td>
        <td data-label="Property">{{ entry.properties | join(", ") }}</td>
        <td data-label="Tiers">{{ entry.tiers | join(", ") or "no" }}</td>
        <td data-label="States">{{ entry.states | join(", ") or "no" }}</td>
        <td data-label="MDN">{% if mdn %}<a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/{{ mdn }}" target="_blank" class="link-mdn" aria-label="MDN reference for {{ mdn }}"></a>{% endif %}</td>
      </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Generated from {{ props.generated_from.file }}, SHA-256 {{ props.generated_from.sha256 | truncate(12, true, "") }}, {{ props.props | length }} props.</p>
  {% elif props and props.props %}
  <p>This build has a prop list in schema version {{ props.version }}. This page reads version 1, so it shows no list. Update the page.</p>
  {% else %}
  <p>This build has no prop list. Run make core_publish to generate it.</p>
  {% endif %}
  {% endblock %}
</article>